/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Press a quartz plate between two metal electrodes and charge appears on the electrodes.
   - Q is the charge in coulombs (C); d is the charge coefficient in C/N, often quoted in pC/N; F is the applied force in newtons (N).
   - V is the open-circuit voltage in volts; g is the voltage coefficient in V·m/N, with g = d/ε; ε is the permittivity of the crystal, ε = ε0 εr, with ε0 = 8.85×10⁻¹² F/m and εr the relative permittivity (4.5 for quartz); t is the plate thickne
   - The coefficient depends on the crystal cut and on the direction of force.
   - Electrically the crystal is a charge source q in parallel with its own capacitance Cc and a leakage resistance R.
   - fL is the low-frequency cut-off in hertz; R is the leakage resistance in ohms; Cc is the crystal capacitance in farads.
   - The charge leaks through R with time constant τ = R Cc.
   - A plain voltage amplifier measures V = q/(Cc + Ck + Cin), where Ck is cable capacitance and Cin is amplifier input capacitance.
   - A charge amplifier avoids this.
   - Vout is the amplifier output voltage in volts; q is the charge from the crystal in coulombs; Cf is the feedback capacitance in farads.
*/
