/* book Worked example: Q: A quartz disc has area 2 cm², thickness 1 mm, d = 2.3 pC/N and εr = 4.5. A dynamic force of 10 N is applied along the sensitive axis. (a) Find the charge produced. (b) Find the crystal capacitance. (c) Find the open-circuit voltage. (d) The disc is read through a cable of 100 pF by a charge amplifier with Cf = 100 pF. Find the output. (e) Find what a plain voltage amplifier would read through the same cable. Take ε0 = 8.85×10⁻¹² F/m.
   1. Convert area and thickness: A = 2 cm² = 2×10⁻⁴ m²; t = 1 mm = 1×10⁻³ m.
   2. Charge: Q = d F = 2.3 pC/N × 10 N = 23 pC = 23×10⁻¹² C.
   3. Capacitance: Cc = ε0 εr A / t = 8.85×10⁻¹² F/m × 4.5 × 2×10⁻⁴ m² ÷ 1×10⁻³ m = 7.965×10⁻¹² F ≈ 7.97 pF.
   4. Open-circuit voltage: V = Q / Cc = 23×10⁻¹² C ÷ 7.965×10⁻¹² F = 2.89 V ≈ 2.9 V.
   5. Charge amplifier: Vout = −q / Cf = −23×10⁻¹² C ÷ 100×10⁻¹² F = −0.23 V. The 100 pF cable does not appear in this result.
   6. Voltage amplifier: the charge now spreads over Cc + Ck = 7.97 + 100 = 107.97 pF, so V = 23 pC ÷ 107.97 pF = 0.213 V. The cable has cut the 2.89 V open-circuit signal by a factor of 2.89 / 0.213 = 13.6, and a longer cable would cut it further.
   Answer: The charge is 23 pC, the crystal capacitance is 7.97 pF and the open-circuit voltage is 2.9 V. The charge amplifier gives −0.23 V whatever the cable; a voltage amplifier through 100 pF of cable reads only 0.213 V. */
