/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Sound is a pressure wave.
   - The medium sets the speed v.
   - λ is wavelength in metres, v is speed of sound in m/s, f is frequency in Hz.
   - Short wavelength sees fine detail.
   - Pulse-echo sends a short burst, stops transmitting and listens.
   - d is distance to target in metres, v is speed in m/s, t is round-trip time in seconds.
   - The crystal is wider than a wavelength, so the beam leaves as a cone.
   - θ is half-angle in radians, λ is wavelength in metres, D is crystal diameter in metres.
   - Range resolution is set by pulse length.
   - Most ultrasonic transducers are piezoelectric (see piezoelectric transducers).
*/
