/* book Worked example 2: Q: A 5 MHz Doppler probe is held on the skin with its beam at 60° to an artery. The speed of sound in tissue is 1540 m/s and the measured frequency shift is 1.3 kHz. Find the speed of the blood.
   1. List the data: f = 5 MHz = 5 × 10⁶ Hz, Δf = 1.3 kHz = 1300 Hz, c = 1540 m/s, α = 60°, so cos α = 0.5.
   2. Start from Δf = 2 f v cos α / c and rearrange: v = Δf c / (2 f cos α).
   3. Substitute: v = 1300 × 1540 / (2 × 5 × 10⁶ × 0.5) = 2.002 × 10⁶ / 5 × 10⁶ = 0.4004 m/s.
   4. Note that a 1.3 kHz shift is in the audible range, which is why a Doppler unit can play the blood flow as a sound.
   Answer: The blood moves at about 0.40 m/s. */
